from pathlib import Path
from runpy import run_path
g=run_path(str(Path(__file__).with_name('author-tools-generator.py')))
L=g['lesson']; C=g['course']
def J(*a): return L(*a,language='javascript')
def T(*a): return L(*a,language='typescript')

C('software-testing','Software testing checks whether a program behaves as expected. We will use tiny examples to practise choosing inputs, checking results and explaining bugs.','The JavaScript examples run in this browser. The text examples are test plans you can work through on paper.','https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Extensions/Testing',[
J('Expected and actual results','A test compares an expected result with the actual result. A passing test only checks the case you gave it.','const add = (a, b) => a + b;\nconsole.log(add(2, 3) === 5);','The result is true: the actual answer matches 5. This does not prove every possible input works.','Try adding zero and write the expected answer first.','One passing example is not proof that a whole program has no bugs.','What does true tell us here?','This example passed','Every possible example passed'),
J('Choose useful cases','A test case includes inputs and the expected result. Choose cases that exercise different rules.','const isFree = age => age < 5;\nconsole.log(isFree(3), isFree(8));','The output is true false. The examples cover both sides of the age rule.','Check ages 4 and 5 too.','Do not test only many inputs that all take the same path.','Which pair covers both free and paid entry?','3 and 8','1 and 2'),
J('Boundaries catch small mistakes','A boundary is where a rule changes. Values just before, on and after it are especially useful tests.','const canEnter = age => age >= 10;\nconsole.log(canEnter(9), canEnter(10), canEnter(11));','The output is false true true. Age 10 is allowed because the rule includes equality.','Change >= to > and spot which case fails.','A greater-than sign and a greater-than-or-equal sign do different jobs.','Which age is exactly on the boundary?','10','11'),
J('Empty input is a real case','An empty list has no items. Decide what your program should do with it before writing a test.','const total = values => values.reduce((sum, value) => sum + value, 0);\nconsole.log(total([]));','The result is 0. The starting value lets reduce handle an empty array.','Try a one-item list.','Without the starting value, reduce on an empty array throws an error.','What result does the empty list produce?','0','An automatic random value'),
J('Negative tests','A negative test checks how a program rejects an invalid input. The expected result may be a useful error instead of a normal value.','function squareSize(side) {\n  if (side < 0) throw new Error("Side must not be negative");\n  return side * side;\n}\ntry { squareSize(-2); } catch (error) { console.log(error.message); }','The output explains that a negative side is not allowed. Rejecting it is the expected behaviour.','Test side zero and explain why it is a different case.','Do not call an expected rejection a failing test.','What does a negative test check?','Handling of invalid input','Only negative numbers in every program'),
L('Unit and integration tests','A unit test checks a small part. An integration test checks parts working together. Each finds different mistakes.','Unit: price(2, 3) returns 6\nIntegration: basket passes its items to the price calculator\nEnd-to-end: a learner adds an item and sees the total','The tests grow from one calculation to a whole visible journey.','Classify a test that checks a form sends values to its validator.','Do not replace every small test with a slow whole-app test.','Which test checks parts working together?','Integration test','A spelling check only'),
L('Make a bug report useful','A bug report should let another person repeat the problem. Include steps, expected behaviour and actual behaviour.','Steps: open quiz, answer 2 questions, press Next\nExpected: question 3 appears\nActual: question 2 stays visible\nBrowser: the browser used for the test','These details give someone a clear starting point. Personal details are not needed.','Write a report for a button that does nothing.','A report that only says broken does not explain how to reproduce the issue.','Which detail helps someone reproduce a bug?','The exact steps','A private password'),
L('Regression tests protect fixes','A regression happens when something that worked stops working. Add a test for a fixed bug so later changes check it again.','Bug: empty basket crashed\nFix: use zero as the starting total\nRegression test: empty basket total equals zero','The test records the behaviour that should stay fixed.','Describe a regression test for a repaired Next button.','Do not remove the test as soon as the bug is fixed.','Why keep a regression test?','To catch the same problem returning','To make a bug permanent'),
J('Repeatable tests','A repeatable test gives the same result with the same inputs. Pass changing values in instead of hiding the current time in the calculation.','const isLate = (due, today) => today > due;\nconsole.log(isLate(5, 6));','The result is always true for these inputs. The test does not depend on today\'s real date.','Try today equal to due.','A test that depends on the real clock can change overnight.','What makes this test repeatable?','The date-like values are supplied','It uses the current time secretly'),
L('Explore with a question','Exploratory testing uses a goal and careful observation. You choose the next step from what you discover.','Goal: can I use the search with only a keyboard?\nTry: Tab to search, type a word, press Enter\nRecord: focus order, result, unexpected behaviour','The exploration has a clear purpose and written observations.','Explore what happens when search has no matches.','Random clicking without notes makes useful findings hard to repeat.','What guides exploratory testing?','A question and observations','Only random clicks'),
L('Accessibility is part of quality','Accessibility means people with different needs can use the app. Test labels, keyboard access and visible focus as well as mouse clicks.','Input label: Search lessons\nKeyboard: Tab reaches input; Enter submits\nFocus: a visible border marks the active control','A learner can understand and operate the search without a mouse. Automatic checks help, but people still need to review it.','Check a form using only Tab, Shift+Tab and Enter.','A color change alone may not explain an error to every learner.','What should a keyboard user be able to see?','Which control has focus','Only the mouse pointer'),
L('Report what was checked','A test summary says what ran, what passed and what remains unchecked. It should not promise more than the evidence supports.','Checked: search, lesson links, quiz scoring\nPassed: 14 cases\nFailed: 1 keyboard case\nNot checked: old browser versions','The summary tells others both the useful evidence and its limits.','Write a two-line summary for three tests you tried.','Do not say all browsers work when only one browser was checked.','What belongs in a test summary?','Uncovered areas as well as results','Only the good news'),
])

C('unit-testing','Unit tests check small pieces of code. We will practise using simple JavaScript functions and explicit checks before moving to a separate test runner.','These examples use console.log so they run here. In a real project, a test runner collects tests and reports failures for you.','https://nodejs.org/api/test.html',[
J('One small behaviour','A unit is a small piece of behaviour, often a function. Test what it returns for a clear input.','function double(n) { return n * 2; }\nconsole.log(double(4) === 8);','The result is true. This test checks doubling four.','Add a test for doubling zero.','Do not test several unrelated functions in one unclear check.','What behaviour is checked here?','Doubling a number','Saving a whole database'),
J('Arrange act assert','Arrange prepares input. Act runs the code. Assert checks the result. These three steps make a test easy to read.','const input = 3; // arrange\nconst actual = input * input; // act\nconsole.log(actual === 9); // assert','The result is true. The expected answer is written separately from the calculation.','Label the three steps in a test for adding two prices.','Do not calculate the expected result using the same buggy function you are testing.','Which step checks the result?','Assert','Arrange'),
J('Name the rule','A useful test name says the rule and the situation. Someone should understand a failure without opening every line of code.','const title = "empty name gets a friendly greeting";\nconst greet = name => name ? "Hi " + name : "Hello!";\nconsole.log(title, greet("") === "Hello!");','The printed check is true, and the name explains the empty-name case.','Write a test name for a non-empty name.','Names such as test1 hide the reason for the test.','What should a test name explain?','The behaviour and situation','Only the file number'),
J('Compare values carefully','Equality checks need to match the data you care about. Two different objects are not the same object even if their fields match.','const actual = { score: 8 };\nconst expected = { score: 8 };\nconsole.log(actual === expected);\nconsole.log(actual.score === expected.score);','The output is false then true. The objects differ, but the score values match.','Compare two arrays by their values on paper.','Use a test runner\'s deep-value matcher when checking object contents.','Why is the first comparison false?','They are different objects','Eight is not equal to eight'),
J('Boundary cases in a unit','Choose a value where a rule changes. Test both sides so a wrong comparison sign is visible.','const passes = score => score >= 5;\nconsole.log(passes(4) === false, passes(5) === true);','Both checks print true. The score of 5 is enough to pass.','Add a check for score 6.','Testing only a very high score misses a wrong boundary.','What is the passing boundary?','5','4'),
J('Test expected errors','Some functions should reject bad input. Check that the rejection happens and that its message is useful.','function divide(a, b) { if (b === 0) throw new Error("Zero divisor"); return a / b; }\nlet message = "";\ntry { divide(4, 0); } catch (error) { message = error.message; }\nconsole.log(message === "Zero divisor");','The result is true. The function rejected division by zero with the expected message.','Test divide(4, 2) as the normal case.','A test that catches every error but checks nothing can pass for the wrong reason.','What should an error test check?','The expected rejection','Only that some code ran'),
J('Fresh data for each test','Tests should not depend on the order they run. Make fresh input when a function changes its input.','const makeList = () => ["book"];\nconst first = makeList();\nfirst.push("pen");\nconsole.log(makeList().length === 1);','The result is true. A later test can start with a clean one-item list.','Create two lists and change only one.','A shared mutable list can make tests pass alone but fail together.','Why create fresh data?','To keep tests independent','To change the correct answer'),
J('Inject a dependency','A dependency is another function or object that code uses. Passing it in lets a test choose a simple, predictable replacement.','function greetFrom(getName) { return "Hi " + getName(); }\nconsole.log(greetFrom(() => "Asha") === "Hi Asha");','The result is true. The test controls the name without any network request.','Pass a function that returns Ben.','Do not call a real paid service just to test a greeting.','What does the test control?','The getName function','Every website on the internet'),
J('Spies record calls','A spy records how a dependency was used. This helps test actions such as saving a message.','const calls = [];\nconst save = value => calls.push(value);\nfunction storeScore(score, saveScore) { saveScore(score); }\nstoreScore(8, save);\nconsole.log(calls.length === 1 && calls[0] === 8);','The result is true. The save function was called once with 8.','Call storeScore twice and inspect calls.','Checking only call count misses a wrong argument.','What does this spy record?','Values passed to save','The browser history'),
J('Wait for async work','Async work finishes later. A test must wait for its promise before checking the result.','async function getScore() { return 8; }\ngetScore().then(score => console.log(score === 8));','The check prints true after the promise resolves. In a test runner, return or await the promise.','Change the returned score and its expected value.','A test can finish too early if you forget to wait.','What must an async test wait for?','The promise result','A guessed number of seconds'),
J('Use several cases','A table of cases checks the same rule with several inputs. Keep each expected result easy to see.','const cases = [[0, 0], [2, 4], [-3, -6]];\nfor (const [input, expected] of cases) console.log(input * 2 === expected);','Three true values are printed. The cases include zero, a positive number and a negative number.','Add the case [5, 10].','More repeated cases do not help if they all test the same uninteresting path.','Which kind of input is covered by -3?','A negative number','An empty string'),
J('Read a failure before fixing it','A failure is evidence that actual and expected results differ. Check the requirement before changing either the code or the expected answer.','const actual = 2 + 2;\nconst expected = 5;\nconsole.log({ actual, expected, passed: actual === expected });','The result has actual 4, expected 5 and passed false. Here the expected answer is wrong.','Write the correct expectation and explain why it is correct.','Do not change an expectation only to turn the test green.','What should you check first after a failure?','The intended rule','How to hide the failure'),
])

def runner_course(slug, framework, docs, import_line, rows):
    lessons=[]
    for title, idea, body, result, task, warning, q, a, w in rows:
        lessons.append(T(title,idea,import_line+'\n'+body,result,task,warning,q,a,w))
    C(slug,f'{framework} runs automated JavaScript tests. We will write small tests, read their results and learn how to keep tests clear and repeatable.',f'These are test-file examples for a local project with {framework} installed. They do not run in this site\'s plain JavaScript sandbox. Use the official setup guide with a teacher if needed.',docs,lessons)

runner_course('jest','Jest','https://jestjs.io/docs/getting-started','import { test, expect, jest, beforeEach } from "@jest/globals";',[
('Your first Jest test','Jest runs a test function and reports whether its checks pass. expect gives the actual value; a matcher states the expected result.','test("adds two prices", () => { expect(2 + 3).toBe(5); });','This test passes because 2 + 3 equals 5.','Change the expected value to 6 and read the failure.','Save test code in a test file matched by your local Jest setup.','Which matcher compares this number?','toBe','toHaveLength'),
('Compare object contents','toEqual checks values inside objects. This is different from checking whether two names refer to the exact same object.','test("book fields", () => { expect({ title: "Moon" }).toEqual({ title: "Moon" }); });','The test passes because the title values match.','Change the expected title.','Use toBe for object identity only when identity is the behaviour you need.','Which matcher checks object contents?','toEqual','toBe'),
('Check lists and text','A length check and a containment check answer different questions about a list.','test("reading list", () => { const books = ["Moon", "Ocean"]; expect(books).toHaveLength(2); expect(books).toContain("Moon"); });','Both checks pass: there are two books, and Moon is one of them.','Add Zoo and update only the length expectation.','Having the right length does not prove the right values are present.','What does toContain check?','Whether a value is present','Only the list length'),
('Check an error','Pass a function to toThrow so Jest can call it and catch the error.','test("rejects bad age", () => { const reject = () => { throw new Error("Bad age"); }; expect(reject).toThrow("Bad age"); });','The test passes when the function throws an error with the expected message.','Change the message to see a useful failure.','Do not call the throwing function before passing it to expect.','What should expect receive here?','The function','Its already-thrown error'),
('Set up fresh state','beforeEach runs before each test in its scope. Use it to prepare fresh mutable data.','let basket;\nbeforeEach(() => { basket = []; });\ntest("starts empty", () => { expect(basket).toHaveLength(0); });','The basket starts empty for the test. Another test can change its own fresh basket.','Add a second test that pushes one item.','Do not rely on another test to clean up your state.','When does beforeEach run?','Before each test','Only after the whole suite'),
('Use a mock function','jest.fn makes a function that records calls. A mock can stand in for a small dependency.','test("saves a score", () => { const save = jest.fn(); save(8); expect(save).toHaveBeenCalledWith(8); });','The test passes because save was called with 8. No real storage is needed.','Also check toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1).','A mock records calls; it does not prove a real database connection works.','What does jest.fn record?','How the function is called','All network traffic'),
('Choose a mock return value','A mock can return a chosen value so the code under test has predictable input.','test("reads a name", () => { const getName = jest.fn().mockReturnValue("Asha"); expect(getName()).toBe("Asha"); });','The mock returns Asha every time in this example.','Choose Ben as the return value.','Keep the fake behaviour consistent with the real dependency\'s contract.','Which method sets a normal mock return value?','mockReturnValue','mockResolvedValue'),
('Wait for a promise','An async test can await a promise. resolves checks the value with which it finishes successfully.','test("loads a score", async () => { await expect(Promise.resolve(8)).resolves.toBe(8); });','The test waits and then passes because the resolved value is 8.','Use Promise.resolve(9) and update the expected result.','Forgetting await or return can let an async test finish too early.','What does resolves inspect?','A fulfilled promise value','A function name'),
('Check promise rejection','rejects checks a promise that fails. It is useful for an expected error path.','test("reports missing data", async () => { await expect(Promise.reject(new Error("Missing"))).rejects.toThrow("Missing"); });','The rejected promise is expected, so this test passes.','Change the error message.','An expected rejected promise is different from an unhandled error.','Which matcher branch checks rejection?','rejects','resolves'),
('Run a table of cases','test.each runs a test with each row of data. A row contains inputs and expected output.','test.each([[2, 4], [3, 6], [0, 0]])("double %i is %i", (input, expected) => { expect(input * 2).toBe(expected); });','Jest runs three cases for the doubling rule.','Add [-2, -4] as a fourth row.','Keep table rows small enough to understand without a second program.','How many test cases are listed?','3','1'),
('Control a timer','Fake timers let a test move time forward without really waiting. Restore real timers when the test ends.','test("reminder runs later", () => { jest.useFakeTimers(); try { const remind = jest.fn(); setTimeout(remind, 1000); jest.advanceTimersByTime(1000); expect(remind).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1); } finally { jest.useRealTimers(); } });','The reminder runs once when fake time advances by 1000 milliseconds.','Assert it has not run before advancing time.','Leaving fake timers active can affect later tests.','Why restore real timers?','To avoid changing later tests','To make the reminder repeat'),
('Review snapshots thoughtfully','A snapshot stores an expected structure. Review changes instead of accepting a new snapshot blindly.','test("book summary", () => { expect({ title: "Moon", pages: 24 }).toMatchInlineSnapshot(`\n{\n  "pages": 24,\n  "title": "Moon",\n}\n`); });','The test checks a small saved object shape. A changed title should cause a review.','Describe when a changed snapshot would be a bug.','Updating every snapshot without reading it can approve a mistake.','What should you do before updating a snapshot?','Review the changed behaviour','Accept every new result'),
])

runner_course('vitest','Vitest','https://vitest.dev/guide/','import { test, expect, vi, beforeEach, afterEach } from "vitest";',[
('Your first Vitest check','Vitest runs named tests and reports mismatched expectations. It works with JavaScript and TypeScript projects.','test("counts apples", () => { expect(3 + 2).toBe(5); });','This test passes because the total is 5.','Change the expected total and read the mismatch.','These imports belong in a Vitest test file, not a plain browser script.','What is the expected total?','5','32'),
('Object equality','toEqual compares the values inside an object. It is useful when a function returns a new object.','test("returns a point", () => { const point = { x: 2, y: 4 }; expect(point).toEqual({ x: 2, y: 4 }); });','The test passes because both coordinates match.','Change y in the expected object.','Do not confuse equal fields with the same object identity.','Which coordinate holds 4?','y','x'),
('Partial object checks','objectContaining checks selected fields when other fields are not part of the current test.','test("has a title", () => { expect({ title: "Moon", pages: 24 }).toEqual(expect.objectContaining({ title: "Moon" })); });','The title is checked. The pages field is allowed but not checked by this expectation.','Add an author to the actual object.','A partial match does not prove unmentioned fields are correct.','Which field is checked?','title','pages'),
('Parameterized tests','test.each repeats one rule for a table of examples. This reduces repeated test code while keeping cases visible.','test.each([[1, 1], [2, 4], [3, 9]])("square %i", (input, expected) => { expect(input * input).toBe(expected); });','Three square-number cases run.','Add [0, 0].','A test table should include useful edge cases, not only easy positive inputs.','How many cases run here?','3','9'),
('Fresh fixtures','A fixture is prepared data used by a test. beforeEach can give each test a new fixture.','let names;\nbeforeEach(() => { names = ["Asha"]; });\ntest("one learner", () => { expect(names).toHaveLength(1); });','The list starts with one learner for each test.','Add a test that pushes Ben and checks length 2.','Sharing changed fixture data can make test order matter.','Why recreate names?','To give each test fresh data','To make every test use more memory forever'),
('Function spies','vi.fn creates a spy that records its calls. You can check the arguments as well as the count.','test("notifies with score", () => { const notify = vi.fn(); notify(7); expect(notify).toHaveBeenCalledWith(7); });','The call with 7 is recorded.','Check that notify ran exactly once.','A spy should replace a clear boundary, not every line of your own function.','Which value was passed to notify?','7','0'),
('Restore a spied method','vi.spyOn watches an existing method. Restore it after the test so later code sees the normal method again.','afterEach(() => { vi.restoreAllMocks(); });\ntest("reads the total", () => { const basket = { total: () => 4 }; vi.spyOn(basket, "total").mockReturnValue(9); expect(basket.total()).toBe(9); });','The spied method returns 9 during the test. Cleanup restores the original method.','Explain what total returned before the spy.','Clearing recorded calls is not the same as restoring an original method.','Which action restores original methods?','restoreAllMocks','Only clearing a local array'),
('Async assertions','Use await when checking a promise. The test must stay active until the check is finished.','test("reads a label", async () => { const load = async () => "ready"; await expect(load()).resolves.toBe("ready"); });','The promise resolves to ready, so the test passes.','Return a different label and update the expectation.','An unawaited promise can hide a failure after the test finishes.','Why use await?','To wait for the assertion','To skip the assertion'),
('Expected rejected work','A failing promise can be the correct result for invalid input. rejects tests that case explicitly.','test("missing book", async () => { await expect(Promise.reject(new Error("No book"))).rejects.toThrow("No book"); });','The test passes when the promise rejects with the expected error.','Check a different error message.','Do not turn all errors into success just to make a test pass.','Is this rejection expected?','Yes','No, every rejection is a test bug'),
('Fake time','Vitest can replace timers with a controlled clock. This makes a timer test quick and repeatable.','test("waits for reminder", () => { vi.useFakeTimers(); try { const remind = vi.fn(); setTimeout(remind, 500); vi.advanceTimersByTime(500); expect(remind).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1); } finally { vi.useRealTimers(); } });','The fake clock reaches 500 milliseconds and triggers the reminder once.','Advance only 499 milliseconds and check no call happened.','Fake timers should be restored even when an assertion throws.','What triggers the reminder here?','Advancing fake time','A real half-second sleep'),
('DOM tests need an environment','A Node test has no real browser page. Choose a DOM test environment or browser mode when the behaviour needs page elements.','// Run in a Vitest DOM environment, such as jsdom.\ntest("button text", () => { const button = document.createElement("button"); button.textContent = "Save"; expect(button.textContent).toBe("Save"); });','With a DOM environment configured, the test creates a button and checks its text.','Change the text to Done.','A DOM simulation does not prove visual layout in a real browser.','What does this test require?','A DOM-capable environment','Only a calculator'),
('Use coverage as a clue','Coverage reports which code was reached by tests. Reaching a line does not prove its result was checked well.','test("both signs", () => { const positive = n => n > 0; expect(positive(2)).toBe(true); expect(positive(-1)).toBe(false); });','Both checks state a result. A coverage report is useful alongside these expectations.','Add the boundary value zero.','High coverage is not a guarantee of correct behaviour.','What does coverage measure?','Which code tests reached','Whether every possible bug is gone'),
])
